lationship Classperformance Discussions (Part 1) available 11 Case studies on Blackboard - Subjects of civil law (Level I - evaluations - Property ownership Introduced) Civil Code 2015 - Civil transactions Tests Civil Law (Part 2) Peer evaluations PPT– Civil Law - Definition of contract 1-5 Classperformance Discussions (Part 2) available 12 - Formation of contract (Level M - evaluations Case studies on Blackboard - Validity of contract Mastery) - Liability for breach of contract Civil Code 2015 Week Topic CLO Assessments Learning Resources activities Tests PPT– Civil Law Civil Law (Part 3) Peer evaluations 1-5 Discussions (Part 3) available - Overview of inheritance Classperformance 13 (Level M - Case studies on Blackboard - Testamentary inheritance Mastery) evaluations - Inheritance at law Civil Code 2015 Labour Law - Definition, nature of labour law PPT – Labour Law - Subjects of labour law 2019 (amended 2025) available on - Employment contracts 14 Quizzes Projects Blackboard - Working & resting time - Salary (incl. salary for Labour Law 2019 overtime working hours) (amended 2025) - Labour disciplines - Dispute settlements 15 Review for Final examination Test 2 Assessment plan Assessment Type',NULL,NULL,NULL,6),(9769,3738,'CLO1','Listen to part of a lecture and decide whether these statements are TRUE or 2 FALSE. (30 pts) 2 Listen to a talk and fill in the summary notes (50 pts) 2 3 Write a short paragraph summarizing the main ideas. (20 pts.) 3 Evaluative criteria for Part 3 1. Content accuracy (16 pts): All statements are accurate and relevant. Summary includes main idea and important details of the given lecture. 2. Organization & Grammar (4 pts): The summary is in the form of a paragraph. No/minor punctuation/grammatical/spelling errors. Revised date: April 22nd, 2026 Ho Chi Minh City, April 29th, 2026 Vice Dean of School of Languages Dr. Vu Hoa Ngan',NULL,NULL,NULL,1),(9770,3738,'CLO2','Demonstrate appropriate listening strategies and note-taking skills, using digital tools to understand and organize information from academic lectures.',NULL,NULL,NULL,2),(9771,3738,'CLO3','CLO4 Ongoing assessment Attendance e- Part 1 Quiz e-Portfolio Part 2 Quiz (30%) Porfolio 60% Pass 60% Pass 60% Pass 60% Pass Part 1 & 2 Midterm test (20%) 60% Pass Part 1& 2 Part 3 Final exam (50%) 60% Pass 60% Pass Note: %Pass: Target that % of students having scores greater than 50 out of 100. 5. Rubrics 5.1. Rubrics for Listening e-Portfolio The portfolio is designed to assess students’ ability to comprehend academic spoken texts, apply listening and note-taking strategies, revise notes for clarity and accuracy, and use digital tools effectively in note preparation and organization. Students compile a digital portfolio of notes based on selected academic listening tasks completed during the course. The portfolio includes original and revised notes from 4 authentic online lectures or talks on course- relevant topics. Each talk should be approximately 5-10 minutes in length and selected from approved academic or educational sources such as TED-Ed, TED Talks, university lecture clips, or other instructor-approved materials. For each entry, students are required to: • provide the title and link to the listening text; • prepare a set of original notes taken while listening; • submit a revised version of the notes after review or replay; • write a brief reflection (approximately 80-120 words) on listening difficulties, strategies used, and how the notes were improved; • organize all entries clearly using an approved digital tool such as Word or Google Docs. Students must submit draft entries at scheduled checkpoints (Week 4 and 10) for feedback and submit the complete portfolio in Week 15; only the final submission is graded. Criteria Description Point CLO s Listening Artifacts Selection of relevant listening materials 20 2 Reflection on Reflection on listening strategies, difficulties, and 20 4 Learning improvement Listening Accuracy and completeness of notes and 35 2 Comprehension and summaries based on listening tasks Notes Digital Note Effective use of digital tools to create, revise, 25 2 Preparation and organize, and present notes and summaries Organization 89 5.2. In-class Listening Quiz Part Task',NULL,NULL,NULL,3),(9772,3738,'CLO4','Display discipline, responsibility, and ethical practices as an individual and a team member in attending class regularly, participating in class activities, and engaging in digital learning. Content The description of the contents should clearly indicate the weighting of the content and the level. Weight: lecture session (2 hours) Topic Weight Level Orientation & Introduction of strategies and 3 I, T, techniques in note-taking U Chapter 1: New Trends in Marketing Research 3 T, U Chapter 2: Business Ethics 3 T, U Chapter 3: Trends in Children’s Media Use 2 T, U Chapter 4: The Changing Music Industry 2 T, U Chapter 5: The Placebo Effect 2 T, U Midterm Sample Test & Review 3 T, U Chapter 6: Intelligent Machines 3 T, U Chapter 7: Sibling Relationships 3 T, U Chapter 8: Multiple Intelligences 3 T, U Final Sample Test & Review 3 T, U Examination Written tests: True/ False; Multiple-choice items (questions and forms statements); summary-note completion 85 Study and Attendance examination Regular on-time attendance in this course is expected. It is compulsory that requirements students attend at least 80% of the course to be eligible for the final exam. Missed tests Students are not allowed to miss any of the tests. There are very few exceptions. (Only with extremely reasonable excuses, e.g. certified paper from doctors, may students re-take the tests.) Class behavior Students are supposed to: • prepare thoroughly for each class in accordance with the syllabus and complete all assignments upon the instructor’s request • participate fully and constructively in all class activities (and discussions if any) • display appropriate courtesy to all involved in the class • provide constructive feedback to faculty members regarding their performance Assignments/Examination: Students must have more than 50/100 points overall to pass this course. Reading list Textbooks [1] Frazie, L., & Leeming, S. (2013). Lecture ready 3. Oxford: Oxford University Press. [2] Rogers, M., Taylore-Knowles, S., & Taylore-Knowles, J. (2018). Skillful: Listening and speaking 3 (2nd ed.). Macmillan Education. References: [3] Lansford, L., Jeffries, A, Barber, D., Dellar, H, & Walkley, A. (2018). Perspective 3. National Geographic Learning. [4] Authentic lecture videos (TED, EnglishBreakingNews, university lectures) 2. Learning Outcomes Matrix (optional) 3. Planned learning activities and teaching methods Wee CL Learning Topic Assessments Resources k O activities COURSE Lecture Ongoing ORIENTATION 1, 2, Group assessment Designed by 1 e-Portfolio Guidance 4 work Midterm lecturer exam 86 Individual Final exam task Chapter 1 New Trends in Lecture Ongoing Marketing Research Group assessment Recognizing topic introducing 1, 2, [1] p.2-13 2 work Midterm and lecture plan presenting 4 Individual exam expressions task Final exam Organizing ideas by outlining Chapter 2 Business Ethics Lecture Ongoing Recognizing transition Group assessment [1] pp. 14- 1, 2, 3 expressions Using symbols work Midterm 25 4 and abbreviations Individual exam [2] 136-142 task Final exam 1, 2, Lecture Ongoing REVIEW 4 Group assessment Designed by 4 e-Portfolio Progress Check work Midterm lecturer (Entry 1) Individual exam task Final exam Chapter 3 1, 2, Lecture Ongoing Trends in Children’s 4 Group assessment [1] p.28-39 5 Media Use work Midterm [2] p.46-55 Recognizing generalization and Individual exam support expressions task Final exam Chapter 4 1, 2, The Changing Music 4 Lecture Ongoing Industry Group assessment Recognizing expressions for 6 work Midterm [1] p.40-52 clarification or emphasis Individual exam Organizing notes by using a task Final exam split-page format 1, 2, Lecture Ongoing REVIEW 4 Group assessment SAMPLE MIDTERM Designed by 7 work Midterm EXAM + lecturer Individual exam CORRECTION task Final exam MIDTERM EXAM 8 87 Chapter 5 1-4 Lecture The Placebo Effect Group Recognizing cause and work Ongoing [1] p.54-65 9 effect expressions Individual assessment [2] p.100- Noting causes and task Final exam 107 Effects Chapter 6 Intelligent 1-4 Lecture Ongoing Machines Recognizing Group assessment expressions used to predict work Final exam [1] p.66-78 10 causes and effects Using Individual [2] p.33-34 arrows to show the task relationship between causes and effects 1-4 Lecture Ongoing REVIEW Group assessment Designed by e-Portfolio Progress Check work Final exam 11 lecturer (Entry 2) Individual task Chapter 7 Sibling 1-4 Lecture Ongoing Relationships Recognizing Group assessment expressions of comparison work Final exam 12 and contrast [1] p.80-91 Individual Noting comparison and task contrast Chapter 8 Multiple 1-4 Lecture Ongoing Intelligences Recognizing Group assessment [1] p.92- non-verbal signals indicating work 13 Final exam 104 important information Individual [2] p.120- Representing information in task 125 list form 1-4 Lecture Ongoing Group assessment REVIEW Designed by 14 work Final exam In-class Listening Quiz lecturer Individual task Lecture Ongoing e-Portfolio Submission Group assessment Designed by 15 SAMPLE FINAL EXAM + 1-4 work Final exam lecturer CORRECTION Individual task FINAL EXAM 88 4. Assessment plan Assessment Type',NULL,NULL,NULL,4),(9773,3739,'CLO1','Identify different types of sentences and mistakes that can make a sentence grammatically incorrect. Skill',NULL,NULL,NULL,1),(9774,3739,'CLO2','10 pts while the meaning kept the same. Notes: *The students are supposed to use a variety of connecting devices (single word, phrase, clause, or sentence) to show their flexibility and expertise in writing. TASK 2: Write a Process essay (60 points) ANSWERS/ CRITERIA Parts/ CLO Points Language use and Mechanics A wide variety of sentence patterns and vocabulary are presented correctly. Spelling, capitalization, punctuations are correct. 10',NULL,NULL,NULL,2),(9775,3739,'CLO3','sentence. (5) Each paragraph has specific supporting details (fact, examples, etc.) (5) Each paragraph has cohesion and coherence. (5) Conclusion: ANSWERS/ CRITERIA Parts/ CLO Points The conclusion summarizes the main points/paraphrases the thesis statement, begins with a conclusion signal, and leaves the readers with the writer’s thoughts on the topic. (5) Total 60 5.3 FINAL EXAM rubrics: TASK 1: Write sentences as required to rebut a given opposing argument: 30 points Categories & Description Scores CLO Appropriateness: Students use appropriate signal language to introduce and rebut the opposing 10',NULL,NULL,NULL,3),(9776,3739,'CLO4','Display discipline, responsibilities, and ethical practices as an individual and a team member in attending class regularly and actively participating in class activities. Content The description of the contents should clearly indicate the weighting of the content and the level. Weight: lecture session (2 hours) Teaching levels: I (Introduce); T (Teach); U (Utilize) Topic Weight Level Types of Sentences 2 I, T, U Sentence Problems 2 I, T, U The process of Academic Writing 1 I, T, U Paraphrasing 1 T, U From Paragraph to Essay 2 T, U Process Essays 4 T, U Cause/Effect Essays 4 T, U Comparison/ Contrast Essays 4 T, U Argumentative Essays 4 T, U Test, Review & Correction 6 U Examination Essay writing forms Study and Attendance examination Regular on-time attendance in this course is expected. A student will be requirements allowed no more than three absences. It is compulsory that the students attend at least 80% of the course to be eligible for the final examination. 92 Missed Tests Students are not allowed to miss any of the tests (both Mid-term and Final). There are very few exceptions. Only with extremely reasonable excuses (eg. certified paper from doctors), students may re-take the examination. Class Behaviors Students are required to treat their studying in college as a full-time job and spend an adequate amount of time for this Writing AE1 course with approximately 8- 10 hours per week (both in class and self-study). Accordingly, students are supposed to follow the obligations below: - Prepare thoroughly for each class in accordance with the course syllabus and complete home assignments as the instructor’s request. - Participate fully and constructively in all course activities and discussions (if any). - Display appropriate courtesy to all involved in the class. - Provide constructive feedback to faculty members regarding their performance. Plagiarism Students are warned not to copy from other books or from their peers for all assessment tasks. Committing plagiarism will result in 0 point for the task. Students who plagiarize twice will be prohibited from sitting the final examination. Assignments/Examination: Students must have more than 50/100 points overall to pass this course. Reading list Textbook [1] Oshima, A., & Hogue, A. (2017). Longman Academic Writing Series, th Level 4: Essays (5 ed.). New Jersey, NJ: Pearson Longman. References [2] Oshima, A., & Hogue, A. (2006). Longman Academic Writing Series, th Level 4: Essays (4 ed.). New Jersey, NJ: Pearson Longman. [3] Bailey, S. (2021). Academic Writing for University Students. London: Routledge. 2. Learning Outcomes Matrix (optional) 3. Planned learning activities and teaching methods Learning Week Topic CLO activities Assessments Resources 93 Types of sentences Lecture [1] pp. 174-184 1,4 Group On-going 1 - Simple sentences work assessment - Compound sentences Wee Learning Assessment Topic CLO k activities Resources s - Complex sentences Individual - writing Compound-complex senten ces Sentence problems: Lecture [1] pp. 194-202 p Sentences Fragments, Chop Group y 2 Sentences, Stringy Sentence 1,4 work On-going Run-ons and Comma Splices s, assessment Individual writing Using Outside Sources: d [1] pp. 58-65 - P