\":\"2\"},\"5\":{\"clo\":\"CLO1, CLO2\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz / Assignment\",\"resources\":\"\",\"level\":\"T, U\",\"weight\":\"2\"},\"6\":{\"clo\":\"CLO5\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz / Assignment\",\"resources\":\"\",\"level\":\"T\",\"weight\":\"1\"},\"7\":{\"clo\":\"CLO5, CLO2\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz / Assignment\",\"resources\":\"\",\"level\":\"T\",\"weight\":\"1\"},\"8\":{\"clo\":\"CLO2\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz / Assignment\",\"resources\":\"\",\"level\":\"T\",\"weight\":\"1\"}},\"plannedActivities\":[{\"week\":1,\"topic\":\"History of computers, relations of software and hardware components\",\"clo\":\"CLO1\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz / Assignment\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":2,\"topic\":\"Assembly language instructions\",\"clo\":\"CLO3\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz / Assignment\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":3,\"topic\":\"Computer arithmetic principles and hardware design\",\"clo\":\"CLO2\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz / Assignment\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":4,\"topic\":\"Midterm\",\"clo\":\"\",\"assessments\":\"\",\"learningActivities\":\"\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":5,\"topic\":\"Computer performance\",\"clo\":\"CLO4\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz / Assignment\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":6,\"topic\":\"Datapath and its control\",\"clo\":\"CLO1, CLO2\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz / Assignment\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":7,\"topic\":\"Microprocessor pipelining\",\"clo\":\"\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz / Assignment\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":8,\"topic\":\"Memory hierarchy\",\"clo\":\"CLO5\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz / Assignment\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":9,\"topic\":\"I/O devices and buses\",\"clo\":\"CLO2\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz / Assignment\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":10,\"topic\":\"Multiprocessor\",\"clo\":\"CLO2\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz / Assignment\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":11,\"topic\":\"Final exam\",\"clo\":\"\",\"assessments\":\"\",\"learningActivities\":\"\",\"resources\":\"\"}],\"assessmentCloMatrix\":{\"0\":{\"CLO1\":\"70.0\",\"CLO2\":\"70.0\",\"CLO3\":\"25.0\"},\"1\":{\"CLO3\":\"50.0\",\"CLO4\":\"70.0\",\"CLO5\":\"70.0\"},\"2\":{\"CLO1\":\"30.0\",\"CLO2\":\"30.0\",\"CLO3\":\"25.0\",\"CLO4\":\"30.0\",\"CLO5\":\"30.0\"}},\"readings\":[{\"title\":\"Computer Organization and Design 5th\",\"author\":\"David A. Patterson and John L. Hennessy\",\"publisher\":\"\",\"year\":\"2013\",\"usageType\":\"REQUIRED\"}],\"assessmentPassNote\":\"%Pass: Target that % of students having scores greater than 50 out of 100. 1. When calculating contact time, each contact hour is counted as a full hour because the organisation of the schedule, moving from room to room, and individual questions to lecturers after the class, all mean that about 60 minutes should be counted.↩︎\",\"contentNote\":\"The description of the contents should clearly indicate the weighting of the content and the level. Weight: lecture session (3 hours) Teaching levels: I (Introduce); T (Teach); U (Utilize)\"}','2026-09-15 04:05:15','2026-09-15 04:07:24','This course introduces the principles of computer organization and the basic computer architecture.','Compulsory','Semester 4','English','Compulsory (CS, NE, CE)','Lecture, lesson, project, seminar.','195','45 (lecture) + 30 (laboratory)','120','Digital Logic Design','This course provides students the principles of computer architecture and organization. It covers the subjects on assembly language and machine code, computer arithmetic and ALU design, computer performance, datapath and control, pipelining, memory hierarchy, I/O devices, multi- processor architectures, and mobile and multi-core processors. Course learning CLO 1. Understand the principles of computer architecture and the outcomes interfaces between its hardware and software components; CLO 2. Understand computer arithmetic (both integer and floating point), datapath, control , pipelining, pipeline hazards and their remedies, computer buses and I/O peripherals, and multiprocessor architecture; CLO 3. Create assembly programs and their machine code equivalent; CLO 4. Analyze the performance of computer; CLO 5. Analyze computer memory and its organization, especially the interaction between cache and main memory. Competency level Course learning outcome (CLO) Knowledge CLO1, CLO2 Skill CLO3, CLO4, CLO5 Attitude Content The description of the contents should clearly indicate the weighting of the content and the level. Weight: lecture session (3 hours) Teaching levels: I (Introduce); T (Teach); U (Utilize) Topic Weight Level History of computers, relations of software and 1 I hardware components; Assembly language instructions 5 T, U Computer arithmetic principles and hardware design 1 T Computer performance 1 T,U Datapath and its control 2 T Microprocessor pipelining 2 T, U Memory hierarchy 1 T I/O devices and buses 1 T Multiprocessor 1 T Examination forms Multiple-choice questions, short-answer questions Study and examination Attendance: A minimum attendance of 80 percent is compulsory for the requirements class sessions. Students will be assessed on the basis of their class participation. Questions and comments are strongly encouraged. Assignments/Examination: Students must have more than 50/100 points overall to pass this course. Reading list 1. David A. Patterson and John L. Hennessy, Computer Organization and Design 5th, 2013 2.','Multiple-choice questions, short-answer questions','Attendance: A minimum attendance of 80 percent is compulsory for the requirements class sessions. Students will be assessed on the basis of their class participation. Questions and comments are strongly encouraged. Assignments/Examination: Students must have more than 50/100 points overall to pass this course.','{\"rubrics\":[{\"type\":\"GRADING_CHECKLIST\",\"title\":\"Grading checklist for Written Reports\",\"scaleLabels\":[\"Level 1\",\"Level 2\",\"Level 3\",\"Level 4\"],\"criteria\":[{\"criterion\":\"Technical content (60%)\",\"levels\":[\"60\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Abstract clearly identifies purpose and summarizes principal content\",\"levels\":[\"10\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Introduction demonstrates thorough knowledge of relevant background and prior work\",\"levels\":[\"15\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Analysis and discussion demonstrate good subject mastery\",\"levels\":[\"30\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Summary and conclusions appropriate and complete\",\"levels\":[\"5\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Organization (10%)\",\"levels\":[\"10\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Distinct introduction, body, conclusions\",\"levels\":[\"5\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Content clearly and logically organized, good transitions\",\"levels\":[\"5\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Presentation (20%)\",\"levels\":[\"20\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Correct spelling, grammar, and syntax\",\"levels\":[\"10\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Clear and easy to read\",\"levels\":[\"10\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Quality of Layout and Graphics (10%)\",\"levels\":[\"10\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"TOTAL SCORE\",\"levels\":[\"100\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]}]},{\"type\":\"HOLISTIC\",\"title\":\"Holistic rubric for evaluating the entire document, e.g., exercises/quizzes/HW\",\"scaleLabels\":[\"Level 1\",\"Level 2\",\"Level 3\",\"Level 4\"],\"criteria\":[{\"criterion\":\"5\",\"levels\":[\"Demonstrates complete understanding of the problem. All requirements of task are included in response.\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"4\",\"levels\":[\"Demonstrates considerable understanding of the problem. All requirements of task are included.\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"3\",\"levels\":[\"Demonstrates partial understanding of the p