aw on women’s (Level R - Classperformance\",\"clo\":\"\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture, Discussion, In-class Exercise\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":6,\"topic\":\"and its nature and basic (Level R - Classperformance Case studies Constitution 2013\",\"clo\":\"\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture\",\"resources\":\"2013\"},{\"week\":7,\"topic\":\"(Level R - Quizzes Projects\",\"clo\":\"\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz, Lab, Midterm examination\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":8,\"topic\":\"Test 1\",\"clo\":\"\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture, Discussion, In-class Exercise\",\"resources\":\"1\"},{\"week\":9,\"topic\":\"(Level M - Classperformance Case studies\",\"clo\":\"\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture, Discussion, In-class Exercise\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":10,\"topic\":\"criminal law (Level M - Classperformance Case studies\",\"clo\":\"\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture, Discussion, In-class Exercise\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":11,\"topic\":\"Case studies on Blackboard\",\"clo\":\"\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture, Discussion, In-class Exercise\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":12,\"topic\":\"- Formation of contract (Level M - evaluations Case studies on Blackboard\",\"clo\":\"\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture, Discussion, In-class Exercise\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":13,\"topic\":\"(Level M - Case studies on Blackboard\",\"clo\":\"\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":14,\"topic\":\"Quizzes Projects Blackboard\",\"clo\":\"\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":15,\"topic\":\"Review for Final examination Test 2\",\"clo\":\"\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz, Lab, Final examination\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture\",\"resources\":\"2\"}],\"assessmentCloMatrix\":{\"0\":{},\"1\":{},\"2\":{}},\"readings\":[{\"title\":\"The Universal Declaration of Human Rights\",\"author\":\"\",\"publisher\":\"\",\"year\":\"1948\",\"usageType\":\"REQUIRED\"},{\"title\":\"The International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights (ICCPR)\",\"author\":\"\",\"publisher\":\"\",\"year\":\"1966\",\"usageType\":\"REQUIRED\"},{\"title\":\"The Convention on the Rights of the Child (CRC)\",\"author\":\"\",\"publisher\":\"\",\"year\":\"1989\",\"usageType\":\"REQUIRED\"},{\"title\":\"Constitution of Vietnam 2013 (amended in )\",\"author\":\"\",\"publisher\":\"\",\"year\":\"2025\",\"usageType\":\"REQUIRED\"},{\"title\":\"Civil Code of Vietnam 2015 (amended in )\",\"author\":\"\",\"publisher\":\"\",\"year\":\"2025\",\"usageType\":\"REQUIRED\"},{\"title\":\"Criminal Code of Vietnam – 2015 (amended in 2017 and )\",\"author\":\"\",\"publisher\":\"\",\"year\":\"2025\",\"usageType\":\"REQUIRED\"},{\"title\":\"Law on Handling of Administrative Violations 2012 (amended in )\",\"author\":\"\",\"publisher\":\"\",\"year\":\"2025\",\"usageType\":\"REQUIRED\"},{\"title\":\"Labour Code 2019 (amended in )\",\"author\":\"\",\"publisher\":\"\",\"year\":\"2025\",\"usageType\":\"REQUIRED\"},{\"title\":\"Law on anti-corruption 2018 (amended in )\",\"author\":\"\",\"publisher\":\"\",\"year\":\"2025\",\"usageType\":\"REQUIRED\"}],\"assessmentPassNote\":\"\",\"contentNote\":\"\"}','2026-09-15 04:10:24','2026-09-15 04:10:24','Foundation course Course designation Face to face','Compulsory','Semester 2','English','Compulsory','Student-centred approach','60','45 hours','15','N/A','The overarching aims of this course are to: • Provide students with foundational knowledge of human rights and the Vietnamese legal system through the integration of technology and real-world case studies, thereby contributing to social and cultural sustainability. • Enhance students’ awareness of their responsibilities toward others and empower them to actively oppose all forms of legal violations, with particular emphasis on combating corruption 71 across diverse social contexts. • Develop essential skills that enable students to act as ambassadors for social justice, promoting fairness and the protection of equitable rights at both national and global levels. • Equip students with the ability to effectively utilize online legal resources and communication tools to support communities in identifying legal issues and formulating appropriate countermeasures. Course learning Upon the successful completion of this course, students will be able to: outcomes Competency level Course learning outcome (CLO) Knowledge CLO1. Analyse fundamental human rights principles in relation to legal issues in society to promote fairness and social sustainability. CLO1.1 Explain fundamental human rights from both international and Vietnamese legal perspectives CLO1.2 Identify human rights issues and the corresponding responsibilities to protect these rights in order to ensure social fairness. CLO2. Apply knowledge of the Vietnamese legal system to address legal issues in diverse social contexts for sustainable and equitable development. CLO2.1. Apply general principles of state and law to analyse and resolve legal issues in various social contexts. CLO2.2. Apply key legal norms from major branches of law (constitutional, civil, criminal, labor, and administrative law) to solve practical legal problems. Skill CLO3. Communicate legal knowledge effectively to raise awareness of rights and responsibilities, promoting fairness and social justice. CLO4. Utilize information and communication technologies (ICTs) and online legal resources to identify legal issues and develop appropriate solutions in community contexts. Attitude CLO5. Demonstrate ethical responsibility and proactive engagement in promoting social and cultural fairness, including opposing corruption and legal violations. CLO6. Exhibit respect for legal norms and principles that support peaceful coexistence and equitable relationships in diverse social contexts. Content This course provides students with foundational knowledge of human rights and the Vietnamese legal system, integrating theoretical perspectives with practical legal contexts. Students will develop an understanding of their rights 72 and obligations under key areas of Vietnamese law, including constitutional, criminal, administrative, civil, labour, and enterprise law. Through the analysis of real-world legal issues, the course fosters students’ ability to apply legal knowledge to promote fairness, social and cultural sustainability, and equitable rights. In addition, students will be encouraged to critically reflect on their responsibilities in upholding justice, including actively opposing corruption and other legal violations in diverse social contexts. The course also emphasises the development of communication skills and the use of information and communication technologies (ICTs) and online legal resources, enabling students to effectively support communities in identifying legal issues and formulating appropriate responses. Examination forms Multiple choice questions Case-based exams Essay exams Oral exams Study and To pass this course, the students must: examination • Achieve a composite mark of at least 50; and requirements • Make a satisfactory attempt at all assessment tasks (see below). GRADING POLICY Grades can be based on the following: Assignment 20% Midterm examination 30% Final examination 50% Total 100% COURSE POLICIES Attendance Regular and punctual attendance at lectures and seminars is expected in this course. University regulations indicate that if students attend less than eighty percent of scheduled classes they may be refused final assessment. Exemptions may only be made on eligible medical grounds. Workload It is expected that the students will spend at least six hours per week studying this course. This time should be made up of reading, research, working on exercises and problems, and attending classes. In periods where they need to complete assignments or prepare for examinations, the workload may be greater. Over-commitment has been a cause of failure for many students. They should take the required workload into account when planning how to balance study with part-time jobs and other activities. General Conduct and Behaviour The students are expected to conduct themselves with consideration and respect 73 for the needs of fellow students and teaching staff. Conduct which unduly disrupts or interferes with a class, such as ringing or talking on mobile phones, is not acceptable and students will be asked to leave the class. The use of laptops is also encouraged during law lessons only to search for materials online. More information on student conduct is available on the university webpage. Keeping informed The students should take note of all announcements made in lectures or on the course’s Blackboard, and another announced mean of communications. From time to time, the university will send important announcements to their university e-mail addresses without providing a paper copy. The students will be deemed to have received this information. Academic honesty and plagiarism Plagiarism is the presentation of the thoughts or work of another as one’s own. Students are also reminded that careful time management is an important part of the study and one of the identified causes of plagiarism is poor time management. Students should allow sufficient time for research, drafting, and the proper referencing of sources in preparing all assessment items. The university regards plagiarism as a form of academic misconduct and has very strict rules regarding plagiarism. Special consideration Requests for special consideration (for final examination only) must be made to the Office of Academic Affairs within one week after the examination. General policy and information on special consideration can be found at the Office of Academic Affairs. Absence on the Mid-term is not allowed, or in special cases approved by Lecturer can be replaced with relevant Assignment. Meeting up with the lecturers after classes Students must make an appointment via emails if they want to meet up with the lecturer after classes and be on time. If there are any changes to the scheduled time, students must inform the lecturer immediately. Reading list Please note that it is very important to gain familiarity with the subject matter in the readings and cases available on Blackboard and the internet before attendance in classes. Required Course Texts and Materials Legal Texts: 1. The Universal Declaration of Human Rights 1948 2. The International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights 1966 (ICCPR) 3. The Convention on the Rights of the Child 1989 (CRC) 4. Constitution of Vietnam 2013 (amended in 2025) 5. Civil Code of Vietnam 2015 (amended in 2025) 6. Criminal Code of Vietnam – 2015 (amended in 2017 and 2025) 7. Law on Handling of Administrative Violations 2012 (amended in 2025) 8. Labour Code 2019 (amended in 2025) 9. Law on anti-corruption 2018 (amended in 2025) Available at https://thuvienphapluat.vn/ or Blackboard Additional materials provided in Blackboard The lecturer will attempt to make lecture notes and additional reading 74 available on Blackboard. However, this is not an automatic entitlement for students doing this subject. Note that this is not a distance learning course, and you are expected to attend lectures and take notes. This way, you will get the added benefit of class interaction and demonstration. Optional Course Texts and Materials Recommended Internet sites UNCTAD (United Nations Conference on Trade and Development) WTO (World Trade Organization) MOIT - Vietnam (Official website of Ministry of Industry and Trade) MPI - Vietnam (Official website of Ministry of Planning and Investment) Other Resources, Support and Information Additional learning assistance is available for students in this course and will be made available on Blackboard. Academic journal articles are available through connections via the VNU - Central Library. Recommended articles will be duly informed to the students.','Multiple choice questions Case-based exams Essay exams Oral exams Study and To pass this course, the students must: examination • Achieve a composite mark of at least 50; and requirements • Make a satisfactory attempt at all assessment tasks (see below). GRADING POLICY Grades can be based on the following: Assignment 20% Midterm examination 30% Final examination 50% Total 100% COURSE POLICIES Attendance Regular and punctual attendance at lectures and seminars is expected in this course. University regulations indicate that if students attend less than eighty percent of scheduled classes they may be refused final assessment. Exemptions may only be made on eligible medical grounds. Workload It is expected that the students will spend at least six hours per week studying this course. This time should be made up of reading, research, working on exercises and problems, and attending classes. In periods where they need to complete assignments or prepare for examinations, the workload may be greater. Over-commitment has been a cause of failure for many students. They should take the required workload into account when planning how to balance study with part-time jobs and other activities. General Conduct and Behaviour The students are expected to conduct themselves with consideration and respect 73 for the needs of fellow students and teaching staff. Conduct which unduly disrupts or interferes with a class, such as ringing or talking on mobile phones, is not acceptable and students will be asked to leave the class. The use of laptops is also encouraged during law lessons only to search for materials online. More information on student conduct is available on the university webpage. Keeping informed The students should take note of all announcements made in lectures or on the course’s Blackboard, and another