60.0\",\"CLO4\":\"60.0\"},\"1\":{\"CLO3\":\"60.0\",\"CLO4\":\"60.0\"}},\"readings\":[{\"title\":\"S. H. ( ). Academic writing skills for international students (2nd ed.). Bloomsbury Academic\",\"author\":\"Read\",\"publisher\":\"\",\"year\":\"2024\",\"usageType\":\"REQUIRED\"},{\"title\":\"L., & Heasley\",\"author\":\"Hamp-Lyons\",\"publisher\":\"B. ( ). Study writing. Cambridge Unviersity Press. References\",\"year\":\"2006\",\"usageType\":\"REQUIRED\"},{\"title\":\"S. ( ). Academic writing: A handbook for international students (5th ed.). Routledge\",\"author\":\"Bailey\",\"publisher\":\"\",\"year\":\"2018\",\"usageType\":\"REQUIRED\"},{\"title\":\"A., & Shafiei\",\"author\":\"Savage\",\"publisher\":\"M. ( ). Effective academic writing 3 (2nd ed.). Oxford University Press\",\"year\":\"2012\",\"usageType\":\"REQUIRED\"},{\"title\":\"A., & Hogue\",\"author\":\"Oshima\",\"publisher\":\"A. ( ). Writing academic English (5th ed.) Pearson. 2\",\"year\":\"2016\",\"usageType\":\"REQUIRED\"}],\"assessmentPassNote\":\"\",\"contentNote\":\"The description of the contents should clearly indicate the weighting of the content and the level. Weight: lecture session (2 hours) Teaching levels: I (Introduce); T (Teach); U (Utilize)\"}','2026-09-15 04:10:25','2026-09-15 04:10:25','name - (in English) Writing AE2 (Research paper writing) - (in Vietnamese) Viết AE2 (Viết bài nghiên cứu) Course This course develops students’ academic writing skills with a focus on designation researchoriented genres in an English-medium academic environment. It introduces essential features of academic writing, including analytical essay writing, the use of sources, and conventions of academic integrity such as referencing, paraphrasing, and summarizing. The course further guides students in identifying the structure and style of a research paper, language features and critical thinking skills. During the semester, students build the ability to analyze, synthesize, and evaluate information effectively in written academic discourses across disciplines.','Compulsory','Semester 2','English / Vietnamese','☒ Compulsory curriculum □ Elective','Lecturers, lesson methods Individual practice Discussion Pair work Group work Project',NULL,NULL,NULL,'- Previous course: Writing AE1 (EN007IU)','to develop students’ knowledge and skills required for effective objectives academic writing in higher education. As writing is part of an integrated skill of reading and writing where reading serves as input to trigger writing, this course is designed to familiarize non-native students with academic writing genres in their field of study by having them read and critically respond to texts of a variety of topics ranging from natural sciences such as biology to social sciences and humanities like education, linguistics and psychology. Students are required to work on the tasks selected to maximize their exposure to written communication and are expected to become competent writers in particular genres across disciplines. Course learning Upon the successful completion of this course students will be able to: outcomes Competency Course learning outcome (CLO) level Knowledge CLO1: Discuss academic writing conventions – essential features of academic writing, and a research paper structure. Skill CLO2: Discuss correct conventions for using sources, paraphrasing and summarizing, text structure and argumentation in common academic writing genres. CLO3: Demonstrate research and analytical writing skills across both manual and digital formats Attitude CLO4: Display discipline, responsibilities, and ethical practices as an individual and a team member by attending class regularly and actively participating in class activities. Content The description of the contents should clearly indicate the weighting of the content and the level. Weight: lecture session (2 hours) Teaching levels: I (Introduce); T (Teach); U (Utilize) Topic Weight Level Introduction to syllabus 1 T Appropriate writing style Developing an academic writing style 1 TU Referencing and using quotations 1 T Paraphrasing and summarizing (1) 1 T Paraphrasing and summarizing (2) 1 TU Plagiarism and how to avoid it 1 T 49 Plagiarism further practice 1 U Midterm review MIDTERM EXAM WEEK OF THE UNIVERSITY The structure of a research paper 1 T Recognizing the structure of a research paper 1 TU Recognizing the structure of a research paper: Further 1 TU Practice 1 T Analytical essays: Structure and style 1 TU Analytical essays: Sample essay analysis 1 U Analytical essays: in-class writing practice Final exam review 1 T Midterm Examination Examination forms Final Examination 50 Study and Attendance: A minimum attendance of 80 percent is compulsory for the class examination sessions. Students will be assessed on the basis of their class participation. requirements Questions and comments are strongly encouraged. Assignments/Examination: Students must have more than 50/100 points overall to pass this course. Plagiarism policy All forms of plagiarism and unauthorized copying are seriously regarded and could result in penalties. Plagiarism occurs when students copy or reproduce people’s words or ideas and then present them as students’ own work without proper acknowledgement, including when students copy the work of their fellow students. Plagiarism in student submissions can be detected by some web-based programs such as SafeAssign, or Turnitin, or examiner\'s judgments with evidence of originals. Penalties due to the degree of similarity include: Degree of similarit Description Description y Below 15% Marked as it is. 16% - 25% The total score is deducted by 25%. 26% - 40% The total score is deducted by 50% Over 40% The total score is 0. Note: Part of the paper is marked as it is if no plagiarism is detected. Policy on AI-generated writing Submitting AI-generated work as if it were the student\'s own is considered academic misconduct. Any violations of this policy, detected using any AIwriting detectors, will lead to penalties. These penalties will only affect the idea development score, as specified below: Degree of similarity Description Below 15% Marked as it is. 16% - 25% The idea development score is deducted by 25%. 26% - 40% The idea development score is deducted by 50% Over 40% The idea development score is 0. Note: Part of the paper is marked as it is if no AI-generated text is detected. 51 Reading list Textbooks [1] Read, S. H. (2024). Academic writing skills for international students (2nd ed.). Bloomsbury Academic. [2] Hamp-Lyons, L., & Heasley, B. (2006). Study writing. Cambridge Unviersity Press. References [3] Bailey, S. (2018). Academic writing: A handbook for international students (5th ed.). Routledge. [4] Savage, A., & Shafiei, M. (2012). Effective academic writing 3 (2nd ed.). Oxford University Press. [5] Oshima, A., & Hogue, A. (2016). Writing academic English (5th ed.) Pearson. 2.','Final Examination 50','Attendance: A minimum attendance of 80 percent is compulsory for the class examination sessions. Students will be assessed on the basis of their class participation. requirements Questions and comments are strongly encouraged. Assignments/Examination: Students must have more than 50/100 points overall to pass this course. Plagiarism policy All forms of plagiarism and unauthorized copying are seriously regarded and could result in penalties. Plagiarism occurs when students copy or reproduce people’s words or ideas and then present them as students’ own work without proper acknowledgement, including when students copy the work of their fellow students. Plagiarism in student submissions can be detected by some web-based programs such as SafeAssign, or Turnitin, or examiner\'s judgments with evidence of originals. Penalties due to the degree of similarity include: Degree of similarit Description Description y Below 15% Marked as it is. 16% - 25% The total score is deducted by 25%. 26% - 40% The total score is deducted by 50% Over 40% The total score is 0. Note: Part of the paper is marked as it is if no plagiarism is detected. Policy on AI-generated writing Submitting AI-generated work as if it were the student\'s own is considered academic misconduct. Any violations of this policy, detected using any AIwriting detectors, will lead to penalties. These penalties will only affect the idea development score, as specified below: Degree of similarity Description Below 15% Marked as it is. 16% - 25% The idea development score is deducted by 25%. 26% - 40% The idea development score is deducted by 50% Over 40% The idea development score is 0. Note: Part of the paper is marked as it is if no AI-generated text is detected. 51','{\"rubrics\":[{\"type\":\"\",\"title\":\"5.1. Ongoing assessment\",\"scaleLabels\":[\"Level 1\",\"Level 2\",\"Level 3\",\"Level 4\"],\"criteria\":[]}]}',NULL,'EN011IU','WRITING AE2',NULL,'CONFIRMED','Hồ sơ CTĐT Khóa 2026 - Khoa học máy tính - v7 - Tổng hợp.pdf','application/pdf','CS-2021','IMPORT_PDF',0),(3742,91,1,'v1.0','CS2026','DRAFT',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"schemaVersion\":1,\"internalNotes\":\"\",\"personResponsible\":\"\",\"dateRevised\":\"May 2025\",\"creditPoints\":\"\",\"lectureCredits\":\"\",\"laboratoryCredits\":\"\",\"workloadStudentResponsibility\":\"\",\"ploCodes\":[\"PLO1\",\"PLO2\",\"PLO3\",\"PLO4\",\"PLO5\",\"PLO6\"],\"cloPloMatrix\":{},\"topicDetails\":{\"0\":{\"clo\":\"\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz / Assignment\",\"resources\":\"\",\"level\":\"I, T\",\"weight\":\"1\"}},\"plannedActivities\":[{\"week\":1,\"topic\":\"Review: Set theory and mapping. Functions and Graphs, Inverse Functions, Exponential and Logarithmic Functions Parametric Curves, Limit. One-sided Limits, Laws of s I, T Limit 1 Evaluating Limits. The Squeeze Theorem. Continuity. 1 T, U The Intermediate Value Theorem Tangent Lines and Velocity Problems. Rates of Change, 1 T, U Derivative. Higher-Order Derivatives, Rules of Differentiation. 1 T, U Rates of Change in the Natural and Social Sciences Implicit Differen