\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture, Discussion, In-class Exercise\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":14,\"topic\":\"Majors and Curriculum, 3, 4 Lecture,\",\"clo\":\"\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture, Discussion, In-class Exercise\",\"resources\":\"\"},{\"week\":15,\"topic\":\"Revision Review-test\",\"clo\":\"\",\"assessments\":\"Quiz, Lab, Final examination\",\"learningActivities\":\"Lecture\",\"resources\":\"\"}],\"assessmentCloMatrix\":{\"0\":{},\"1\":{\"CLO1\":\"25.0\",\"CLO2\":\"25.0\",\"CLO3\":\"25.0\"},\"2\":{\"CLO1\":\"25.0\",\"CLO2\":\"25.0\",\"CLO3\":\"25.0\"}},\"readings\":[{\"title\":\"“Computer science: Illuminated”, 7th Edition, Jones & Bartlett Learning Publisher\",\"author\":\"Nell Dale and John Lewis\",\"publisher\":\"ISBN-13 978- 1284155617\",\"year\":\"2019\",\"usageType\":\"REQUIRED\"},{\"title\":\"“Computer Science: An Overview”, 12th Edition, Pearson Publisher\",\"author\":\"J. Glenn Brookshear\",\"publisher\":\"ISBN-13 978-0133760064\",\"year\":\"2014\",\"usageType\":\"REQUIRED\"},{\"title\":\"Jeffrey Elkner, \\\"How to Think Like a Computer Scientist: Learning with Python 3 Documentation\\\", 3rd Edition, Allen B. Downey and Chris Meyers, Green Tea Press Publisher, ISBN-13 978-0971677500\",\"author\":\"Peter Wentworth\",\"publisher\":\". 2\",\"year\":\"2020\",\"usageType\":\"REQUIRED\"}],\"assessmentPassNote\":\"%Pass: Target that % of students having scores greater than 50 out of 100. 1. When calculating contact time, each contact hour is counted as a full hour because the organisation of the schedule, moving from room to room, and individual questions to lecturers after the class, all mean that about 60 minutes should be counted.↩︎ 5.\",\"contentNote\":\"\"}','2026-09-15 04:10:31','2026-09-15 04:10:31','This course introduces students to a broad knowledge of the designation computer science and information technology fields. Topics covered will include basic computer concepts, components of computer hardware and operating systems software as well as data and telecommunications systems. Students can use the knowledge they\'ve gained to strengthen their future-oriented job. 34','Compulsory','Semester 1','English','Compulsory','Lecture, lesson, project, seminar.','135 hours.','45 hours (lecture).','90 hours.','None','This course is to provide fundamentals and basic concepts of computer science and engineering, basics of Computing such as basic concepts, models, trends in industry. Introduction to majors and curricula, career path of all majors in computing, career orientation, job requirements and career opportunities in industry are also included in this course. Course learning CLO1: Demonstrate an in-depth understanding of fundamental outcomes knowledge and history of computing, all career paths in computing and learning methodologies in university. CLO2: Describe basic hardware and software concepts and basic computing terminologies CLO3: Make a plan for his/her own future career and his/her works CLO4: Seek information from the Internet and manage his/her information. CLO5: Follow the discussions of instructors and classmates. Competency Course learning outcome level (CLO) Knowledge CLO1, CLO2. Skill CLO3, CLO4. Attitude CLO5. Content The description of the contents should clearly indicate the weighting of the content and the level. Weight: lecture session (3 hours) 35 Teaching levels: I (Introduce); T (Teach); U (Utilize) Topic Weigh Leve t l The Overall Picture 1 I Data and Information 2 T, U Hardware 2 T, U Algorithm and Programming Language 2 T, U Operating System 2 T, U Networking 2 T, U Information System and Application 2 T, U Majors and Curriculum, Career Paths and 1 I Orientation Careers at a Hardware, Network and Software Company Revision 1 Examination Multiple-choice questions, short-answer questions forms Study and Attendance: A minimum attendance of 80 percent is compulsory examination for the class sessions. Students will be assessed on the basis of their requirements class participation. Questions and comments are strongly encouraged. Assignments/Examination: Students must have more than 50/100 points overall to pass this course. Reading list [1] Nell Dale and John Lewis, “Computer science: Illuminated”, 7th Edition, Jones & Bartlett Learning Publisher, ISBN-13 978- 1284155617, 2019. [2] J. Glenn Brookshear, “Computer Science: An Overview”, 12th Edition, Pearson Publisher, ISBN-13 978-0133760064, 2014. [3] Peter Wentworth, Jeffrey Elkner, \"How to Think Like a Computer Scientist: Learning with Python 3 Documentation\", 3rd Edition, Allen B. Downey and Chris Meyers, Green Tea Press Publisher, ISBN-13 978-0971677500, 2020. 2.',NULL,NULL,'{\"rubrics\":[{\"type\":\"GRADING_CHECKLIST\",\"title\":\"Grading checklist for Written Reports\",\"scaleLabels\":[\"Level 1\",\"Level 2\",\"Level 3\",\"Level 4\"],\"criteria\":[{\"criterion\":\"Technical content (60%)\",\"levels\":[\"60\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Abstract clearly identifies purpose and summarizes principal content\",\"levels\":[\"10\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Introduction demonstrates thorough knowledge of relevant background and prior work\",\"levels\":[\"15\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Analysis and discussion demonstrate good subject mastery\",\"levels\":[\"30\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Summary and conclusions appropriate and complete\",\"levels\":[\"5\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Organization (10%)\",\"levels\":[\"10\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Distinct introduction, body, conclusions\",\"levels\":[\"5\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Content clearly and logically organized, good transitions\",\"levels\":[\"5\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Presentation (20%)\",\"levels\":[\"20\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Correct spelling, grammar, and syntax\",\"levels\":[\"10\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Clear and easy to read\",\"levels\":[\"10\",\"\",\"\",\"\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Quality of Layout and Graphics (10%)\"